    "collapsible_content_DJhK33": {
      "type": "collapsible-content",
      "blocks": {
        "collapsible_row_fTFJFb": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "1. What are the risks of wearing contact lenses while diving?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Wearing contact lenses in water can lead to severe infections that may cause long-term vision problems or even blindness. The water can introduce bacteria and other pathogens to your eyes, increasing the risk of serious infections such as acanthamoeba keratitis.</p><p></p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_AdWJeG": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "2. Will contact lenses cause discomfort while diving?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Yes, if water gets in your eye, it can irritate and require frequent adjustments of your lenses, disrupting your underwater experience. The constant need to adjust your lenses can be distracting and uncomfortable, preventing you from fully enjoying your dive.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_WpFaxz": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "3. Can contact lenses fog up underwater?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Yes, contacts can fog up or become blurry underwater due to nitrogen bubbles getting trapped behind the lenses. This fogging can obscure your view of the beautiful marine life and make it difficult to see clearly underwater.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_xeTyYj": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "4. Can I lose my contact lenses while diving?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Yes, it is possible. Water can easily dislodge your lenses, leaving you with blurry vision and a ruined snorkeling or diving adventure. Losing a lens can be particularly problematic if you rely on them for clear vision, as it may force you to cut your dive short.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_akCYct": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "5. How difficult is it to find replacement contact lenses while traveling?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Finding replacement lenses in remote locations is challenging and expensive, turning your dream vacation into a stressful hunt. You may not have access to the specific type or prescription of lenses you need, leading to significant inconvenience and expense.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_6Kfxjw": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "6. Do contact lenses require special maintenance during a diving trip?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Yes, contacts need meticulous handling and maintenance, especially when you're on vacation, adding unnecessary hassle to your trip. Proper hygiene is crucial to prevent infections, and maintaining this level of care can be difficult while traveling.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_fcC6cw": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "7. How do contact lenses affect my confidence while diving?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>The fear of losing lenses or developing eye issues can make snorkeling or diving a stressful experience, robbing you of the joy and relaxation you seek. Concerns about your vision and eye health can distract you and reduce your overall enjoyment of the activity.</p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        }
      },
